  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "The heights, in centimeters, of six randomly selected tulips drawn from a population were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$$\n35,\\,37,\\,42,\\,51,\\,48,\\,51\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({X}_{i}-44)^{2}}{n-1}$ for this particular sample.",
  "worked_solution": "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- ${X}_{i}$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- ${x}_{i}$ is the value of ${X}_{i}$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}\\frac{({x}_{i}-44)^{2}}{6-1} & =\\frac{1}{5}((35-44)^{2}+(37-44)^{2}+(42-44)^{2}+(51-44)^{2}+(48-44)^{2}+(51-44)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(81+49+4+49+16+49) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(248) \\\\  & =49.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $49.6\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.$",
